        "fr-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "b982d004-6f4e-3f71-92a2-a3275de2c607",
                "word": "anhédonie",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "dcc368a9-7ff3-4f91-8a97-cb56342d866f",
                        "entry_id": "b982d004-6f4e-3f71-92a2-a3275de2c607",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "anhédonies"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9e7e6ed3-4e05-3006-a15d-07b0b4a9dc2b",
                        "entry_id": "b982d004-6f4e-3f71-92a2-a3275de2c607",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Incapacité à éprouver du plaisir dans des situations ordinaires."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "17359138-f6ac-40a7-a071-c62420ee66a1",
                                "sense_id": "9e7e6ed3-4e05-3006-a15d-07b0b4a9dc2b",
                                "text": "« L’anhédonie, soit la perte de plaisir à faire des activités qui nous font d’habitude du bien, est un des symptômes fondamentaux de la dépression », explique le psychiatre Boris Chaumette."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7a84dfc2-9238-455d-95c9-53c58493a3f9",
                                "sense_id": "9e7e6ed3-4e05-3006-a15d-07b0b4a9dc2b",
                                "text": "C’est ainsi que l’apathie et l’anhédonie ont fait l’objet de nombreux travaux, tout d'abord dans le cadre de la schizophrénie et de maladies neurologiques comme la maladie de Parkinson et la maladie d’Alzheimer, puis plus récemment dans celui de la dépression."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "b245e1c6-5594-4f94-a907-798437ee55f6",
                        "source_entry_id": "b982d004-6f4e-3f71-92a2-a3275de2c607",
                        "source_sense_id": "9e7e6ed3-4e05-3006-a15d-07b0b4a9dc2b",
                        "target_lang_word": "anhedonia",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "anhedonia",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9de60cea-cf5d-3620-8444-1af8a89553e9",
                                        "entry_id": "4c99ceea-4289-3bd5-a5b5-80b560ecbab3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The inability to feel pleasure from activities usually found enjoyable, such as exercise, hobbies, music, sexual activities or social interactions."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c2f21252-7c20-4326-ad71-fafe4a4d8035",
                                                "sense_id": "9de60cea-cf5d-3620-8444-1af8a89553e9",
                                                "text": "Prolonged seasickness will in most persons produce a temporary condition of anhedonia. Every good, terrestrial or celestial, is imagined only to be turned from with disgust."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c3769a14-2d82-4eb7-9444-6237227fe95d",
                                                "sense_id": "9de60cea-cf5d-3620-8444-1af8a89553e9",
                                                "text": "Rather, Meehl suggests that anhedonia is a manifestation of the mixed signals to which the preschizophrenic individual is subjected because of the neurointegrative deficit."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ded9e4a5-ff33-4031-8a14-4923a70a92ee",
                                                "sense_id": "9de60cea-cf5d-3620-8444-1af8a89553e9",
                                                "text": "Secondly, the distinction between consummatory and anticipatory anhedonia [21] is not taken into account although these two anhedonias could play different role in the risk of suicide."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8754adb3-7546-42ce-98c6-211867f3a194",
                                                "sense_id": "9de60cea-cf5d-3620-8444-1af8a89553e9",
                                                "text": "Anhedonia is the reduced ability to experience pleasure from activities that were previously found to be enjoyable. Anhedonia can induce diminished motivation to engage in a potentially pleasurable activity (anticipatory anhedonia) and loss of the enjoyment experienced from the action itself (consummatory anhedonia)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "ba94bbfa-db9d-4b71-b7a3-a54f9845e5d6",
